Reasoning through the schema and ontology:

Entity types present in the schema:
1. The event itself — each row is one conflict event, identified by event_id_cnty (unique and stable per the description). The ontology's event-like class is bsm:Action, which carries eventStartDateTime, actionDescriptionText, classificationCode, involvedActor, hasResult, and (inherited from BattlespaceConcept) hasGeographicLocation, additionalInformationText. Subject template: event_{event_id_cnty}.
2. Actor 1 — actor1 holds a name like "Military Forces of Poland (2023-)" and inter1 holds its type ("State forces"). The ontology has bsm:Actor one hop from Action via bsm:involvedActor, so this must be its own entity, not folded into the event as literals. Values are a mix of organisations, groups, and collectives (Rioters, Civilians), so bsm:Actor (common superclass of Organisation and Person) is the safe class rather than bsm:Organisation. Subject template: actor_{actor1}. Properties: conceptName from actor1, typeName (inherited from bsm:Object) from inter1 — "type of Actor 1" matches "type name".
3. Actor 2 — same structure via actor2/inter2. Subject template: actor_{actor2}. actor2 can be blank, in which case the entity is skipped for that record, which is correct.
4. Location — the location column plus admin1/country give the event's place, and Action inherits bsm:hasGeographicLocation from BattlespaceConcept, so Location is a linked class one hop away and must be its own entity. Subject template: location_{country}_{admin1}_{location} (country and admin1 included so same-named places in different areas do not merge). Property: locationName from location.
5. Geometry — Location has bsm:hasGeometry leading to bsm:Geometry, and latitude/longitude are decimal degrees in EPSG:4326, which matches bsm:AbsolutePoint (latitudeDecimal/longitudeDecimal, WGS84) exactly. Subject template: point_{latitude}_{longitude}.
6. Administrative hierarchy — admin1, country, and region are place names with no dedicated properties in the ontology, but BattlespaceConcept has bsm:partOf, so I model them as bsm:Location entities chained: event location partOf admin1 (admin_{country}_{admin1}), admin1 partOf country (country_{country}), country partOf region (region_{region}). admin2/admin3 are blank in the samples and sparsely populated generally, and templates cannot express conditional fallback parents, so they stay unmapped.
7. Action effect — fatalities is a quantified outcome of the event. Action has bsm:hasResult leading to bsm:ActionEffect, which has effectQuantityNumber. One effect per event: effect_{event_id_cnty}, with effectQuantityNumber from fatalities and a constant effectDescriptionCode of "fatalities" to characterize the quantity (low confidence, no column evidences the code).

Property decisions on the event:
- event_date to bsm:eventStartDateTime. The near-synonym pair eventStartDateTime/eventEndDateTime differs only by the start/end qualifier, and the column ("the date on which the event took place") is silent on which one — this is explicitly ambiguous; I choose start because a single occurrence date is best treated as when the event begins, but end would be equally defensible.
- notes to bsm:actionDescriptionText — description says "a short description of the event", a direct match.
- event_type to bsm:classificationCode — "the type of event" is the event's classification; disorder_type and sub_event_type are the same classification axis at other granularities, and since there is only one classificationCode property I map the primary type field and leave the other two unmapped rather than overload the property.
- tags to bsm:additionalInformationText — "additional structured information about the event" matches "additional information".
- civilian_targeting vs bsm:hostilityCode: hostility code in a battlespace sense (hostile/friendly) is not what "Civilians targeted" encodes, so I leave it unmapped rather than force the near-sounding match.
- source vs bsm:sourceIdentifier: sourceIdentifier is the identifier of the concept in a source system, while the source column lists names of reporting organizations — a semantic mismatch, left unmapped. assoc_actor_1/2 are semicolon-separated lists of co-actors; no "associated actor" property exists and a template cannot split the list into separate IRIs, so unmapped. interaction is a derived label of the two inter types — unmapped.

Unmapped columns: year (redundant with event_date, no year property), time_precision, geo_precision (no precision properties), disorder_type, sub_event_type, assoc_actor_1, assoc_actor_2, interaction, civilian_targeting, iso, admin2, admin3, source, source_scale, timestamp (upload time, not an observation time), population_1km, population_2km, population_5km, population_best (no population properties).

IRI cross-check: every iri-typed value's transformation is copied character-for-character from its target's subject_transformation — event hasGeographicLocation uses location_{country}_{admin1}_{location}; Location hasGeometry uses point_{latitude}_{longitude}; Location partOf uses admin_{country}_{admin1}; admin1 partOf uses country_{country}; country partOf uses region_{region}; involvedActor uses actor_{actor1} and actor_{actor2}; hasResult uses effect_{event_id_cnty}. All class and property URIs were re-checked verbatim against the ontology block. No transformation keys are emitted as empty objects; where a raw column value is used, the key is omitted entirely.

